                "text": "**Analyzing Input Grids**\n\nI'm examining the input's structure, specifically the separating vertical line of \"5\"s. I'm noting the grid dimensions (5x5) and the 0-indexed position of the separator at column 2. That means column 0 and 1 are the left and column 3 is the right. I'm focusing on these dimensions to understand how the data is split.\n\n\n**Revising Output Interpretation**\n\nI'm now wrestling with the output and how it relates to the input. My focus is on understanding the mapping of values, especially concerning the missing elements. I'm scrutinizing row-by-row, questioning whether mirroring, shifting or overlaying is at play, because the position of the values in the output doesn't seem to correlate with either the left or the right side of the input. I'm trying to figure out which input values are showing up in the output.\n\n\n**Interpreting Win Conditions**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the \"winning\" condition between the left and flipped right values. It seems the *smaller* non-zero number is the winner. If one side is zero and the other is not, the non-zero value wins. If both are zero, the output is zero. This seems consistent across examples.\n\n\n**Confirming Dominance Rules**\n\nI am now clarifying the logic when non-zero values exist on both sides. In all examples I've analyzed, it looks like if both sides have non-zero values at a corresponding position, the *left* side \"wins\", or rather, the left side's value is the one that's used. When only one side has a non-zero value, that value is used. If both are zero, the output is zero, and if one side has values, while the other is all zeros, the non-zero side takes over.\n\n\n**Refining Value Overlap**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into how the left and flipped right sides interact, particularly when there are non-zero values. My initial hypothesis of the \"left wins\" rule is looking solid. I'm focusing on the overlay process, picturing the right side folding onto the left.